00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:46,160
PASSAGE 1
To achieve financial security, how much you save is always more important than the amount you earn or how shrewdly you invest.

63
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:53,300
If you’re under 30 years old, your goal should be to save 20% of your monthly income after tax deductions.

64
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:58,780
This is irrespective of how much you earn.

65
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:03,980
Approximately 50% should be reserved for essentials like food and accommodation.

66
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:08,780
The remaining 30% is for recreation and entertainment.

67
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:16,000
But for many young people, it'll be difficult to designate such a large proportion of their income for savings.

68
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:23,040
If you find it hard to save any money at all, start by cutting all unnecessary spending.

69
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:30,660
Allocate a tiny amount of 1 or 2 percent for savings and gradually increase that amount.

70
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:36,700
Always keep that 20% goal in mind to prevents yourself from becoming complacent.

71
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,440
It can be challenging to stick to such a strict plan.

72
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:45,440
But if you adopt the right mindset, you should be apt to make it work for you.

73
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:48,420
So, what should you be doing with the money that you're saving?

74
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:54,540
Some must be kept easily accessible in case you need some cash in an emergency.

75
00:08:54,540 --> 00:09:01,280
The largest proportion should be invested in retirement plans, either through your employer or privately.

76
00:09:01,280 --> 00:09:07,080
And you can keep some money for high-risk but potentially lucrative investments.

77
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:11,740
Dividends can be reinvested or used to purchase something you like.

78
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:25,620
By following this plan, you should hopefully be able to enjoy your life now, and still be financially secure in the future.

79
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:46,280
Q9. What are people under 30 advised to do to achieve financial security?

80
00:09:46,280 --> 00:10:06,840
Q10. What should people do if they find it difficult to follow the speaker’s advice on their financial plan?

81
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:23,920
Q11. What does he speaker think is important for achieving financial security?

82
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:35,020
PASSAGE 2
I work in advertising and I like to keep up with current trends, mainly because I’m aware that we live in an image-obsessed world.

83
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:44,560
However, when I first started my job, occasionally, I'd catch a glimpse of myself in the lifts and find myself thinking that I looked a total mess.

84
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:47,960
Was I being held back by my choice of clothing?

85
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:54,760
The short answer is yes, especially when clients are quick to judge you on your style rather than your work.

86
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:58,040
But no one can be unique with their outfit every day.

87
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:01,240
I mean that’s why uniforms were invented.

88
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:03,340
So, here’s what I did.

89
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:06,260
I created my own uniform.

90
00:11:06,260 --> 00:11:08,700
To do this, I chose an appropriate outfit.

91
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:14,000
Then I bought multiple items of the same style in different shades.

92
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:21,980
Now, I never worry about what I’m wearing in the morning even if I do get a bit tired of just wearing the same classic pieces.

93
00:11:21,980 --> 00:11:29,280
Overall, when it comes to work, you have to ask yourself: “Will looking smarter enhance my ability to do my job?

94
00:11:29,280 --> 00:11:37,980
” For some, this question may not be an issue at all, especially if you work remotely and rarely see your colleagues or clients face to face.

95
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:45,120
But if your job involves interacting with other people, the answer to this is often yes.

96
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:51,520
So rather than fighting the system, I think we should just do whatever helps us to achieve our goals at work.

97
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:55,480
If that means playing it safe with your image, then let’s face it.

98
00:11:55,480 --> 00:12:04,240
It’s probably worth it.

99
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:22,900
Q12. What do we learn about the speaker when she first started her job?

100
00:12:22,900 --> 00:12:41,120
Q13. Why were uniforms invented according to the speaker?

101
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:59,120
Q14. What does the speaker say about looking smarter?

102
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:44,160
Q15. What does the speaker advise people to do in an image-obsessed world?

103
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:51,240
RECORDING 1
Did you know that Americans have approximately three times the amount of space we had 50 years ago?

104
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:56,180
Therefore, you’d think we’d have sufficient room for all of our possessions.

105
00:13:56,180 --> 00:14:01,180
On the contrary, the personal storage business is now a growing industry.

106
00:14:01,180 --> 00:14:09,060
We've got triple the space, but we’ve become such enthusiastic consumers that we require even more.

107
00:14:09,060 --> 00:14:23,220
This phenomenon has resulted in significant credit card debt, enormous environmental footprints, and perhaps not coincidentally, our happiness levels have failed to increase over the same half century.

108
00:14:23,220 --> 00:14:29,480
I’m here to suggest an alternative that having less might actually be a preferable decision.

109
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:33,960
Many of us have experienced, at some stage, the pleasure of possessing less.

110
00:14:33,960 --> 00:14:43,160
I propose that less stuff and less space can not only help you economize, but also simplify your life.

111
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:50,940
I recently started an innovative project to discover some creative solutions that offered me everything I required.

112
00:14:50,940 --> 00:14:59,240
By purchasing an apartment that was 40 square meters, instead of 60, I immediately saved $200,000.

113
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:06,300
Smaller space leads to reduced utility bills, and also a smaller carbon footprint.

114
00:15:06,300 --> 00:15:15,000
Because it is designed around an edited collection of possessions, limited to my favorite stuff, I'm really excited to live there.

115
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,360
How can we live more basically?

116
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:22,540
Firstly, we must ruthlessly cut the unnecessary objects out of our lives.

117
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:30,640
To stem consumption, we should think before we buy and ask ourselves, “Will it truly make me happier?

118
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:33,540
” Obviously, we should possess some great stuff.

119
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:37,960
But we want belongings that we’re going to love for years.

120
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:41,560
Secondly, we require space efficiency.

121
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:46,980
We want appliances that are designed for use most of the time, not for occasional use.

122
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:53,840
Why own a six-burner stove when you rarely use even three burners?

123
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:58,100
Finally, we need multifunctional spaces and housewares.

124
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:04,320
I combined a moveable wall with transforming furniture to get more out of my limited space.

125
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:06,040
Consider my coffee table.

126
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:09,300
It increases in size to accommodate ten.

127
00:16:09,300 --> 00:16:12,940
My office is tucked away, easily hidden.

128
00:16:12,940 --> 00:16:15,300
My bed simply pops out of the wall.

129
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:23,420
For guests, I can relocate the moveable wall and utilize the foldable guest beds I installed.

130
00:16:23,420 --> 00:16:30,440
I’m not saying we should all live in tiny apartments, but consider the benefits of an edited life.

131
00:16:30,440 --> 00:16:38,920
When you return home and walk through your front door, take a moment to ask yourself, “Could I do with a little life editing?

132
00:16:38,920 --> 00:16:46,175
Would that give me more freedom and more time?

133
00:16:46,175 --> 00:16:49,400
”

134
00:16:49,400 --> 00:17:08,380
Q16. What has prevened Americans' happiness levels from increasing?

135
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:26,480
Q17. What things should we possess according to the speaker?

136
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:44,940
Q18. What do we learn about the items in the speaker's home?

137
00:17:44,940 --> 00:17:54,240
RECORDING 2
Now, believe it or not, people sometimes lie in order to maintain a good, honest reputation—even if it hurts them to do so.

138
00:17:54,240 --> 00:18:00,620
At least, this is what a team of scientists is suggesting, with evidence to prove it.

139
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:07,920
Picture this scenario: You often drive for work and can be compensated for up to 400 miles per month.

140
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:12,180
Most people at your company drive about 300 miles each month.

141
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:15,900
But this month, you drove 400 miles.

142
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:19,920
How many miles do you think you'd claim in your expense report?

143
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:24,880
The scientists asked this exact question as part of the study we’re discussing today.

144
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:38,320
With surprising results, they found that 12% of respondents reported the distance they drove as less than the actual figure, giving an average answer of 384 miles.

145
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:44,760
In other words, they lied about the number of miles, even though they would forfeit money they were owed.

146
00:18:44,760 --> 00:18:53,740
The researchers believe this was to seem honest, with the assumption being that others would be suspicious of a high expense claim.

147
00:18:53,740 --> 00:18:57,860
But why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment?

148
00:18:57,860 --> 00:19:05,440
The researchers explain that many people care a great deal about their reputation and how they'll be judged by others.

149
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:14,720
If they care enough, their concern about appearing honest and not losing the respect of others may be greater than their desire to actually be honest.

150
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:30,880
The researchers assert that the findings suggest that when people obtain very favorable outcomes, they anticipate other people’s suspicious reactions and prefer lying and appearing honest to telling the truth and appearing as selfish liars.

151
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:33,620
So why is this research important?

152
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:41,960
Well, experts generally agree there are two main types of lie: selfish lies and lies that are meant to benefit others.

153
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:59,420
The first, as you may predict, is for selfish gain, such as submitting a fraudulent claim to an insurance company, while the second involves lying to help others or not offend others, for example, telling a friend whose outfit you don’t like that they look great.

154
00:19:59,420 --> 00:20:05,860
But the researchers are suggesting a third type of lying—lying to maintain a good reputation.

155
00:20:05,860 --> 00:20:11,800
Now this hypothesis is new and some skeptics argue that this isn’t a whole new category of lie.

156
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:15,120
But the findings seem intuitive to me.

157
00:20:15,120 --> 00:20:37,217
After all, one of the main motivations for lying is to increase our worth in the eyes of others, so it seems highly likely that people will lie to seem honest.

158
00:20:37,217 --> 00:20:50,760
Q19. What did a team of scientists find in their study?

159
00:20:50,760 --> 00:21:10,180
Q20. Why would people fabricate numbers to their own detriment according to the researchers?

160
00:21:10,180 --> 00:21:28,140
Q21. What does the speaker think of the researchers’ findings?

161
00:21:28,140 --> 00:21:31,620
RECORDING 3
Why do old people dislike new music?

162
00:21:31,620 --> 00:21:40,500
As I’ve grown older, I often hear people my age say things like “They just don’t make good music like they used to.

163
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:42,500
” Why does this happen?

164
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:47,100
Luckily, psychology can give us some insights into this puzzle.

165
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:51,600
Musical tastes begin to crystallize as early as age 13 or 14.

166
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:58,620
By the time we’re in our early 20s, these tastes get locked into place pretty firmly.

167
00:21:58,620 --> 00:22:06,060
In fact, studies have found that by the time we turn 33, most of us have stopped listening to new music.

168
00:22:06,060 --> 00:22:14,440
Meanwhile, popular songs released when you’re in your early teens are likely to remain quite popular among your age group through the rest of your life.

169
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:27,780
There could be a biological explanation for this, as there is evidence that the brain’s ability to make subtle distinctions between different chords, rhythms and melodies deteriorates with age.

170
00:22:27,780 --> 00:22:32,429
So to older people, newer, less familiar songs might all “sound the same.

171
00:22:32,429 --> 00:22:33,860
”

172
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:38,540
But there may be some simpler reasons for older people’s aversion to newer music.

173
00:22:38,540 --> 00:22:50,940
One of the most researched laws of social psychology is something called the “mere exposure effect,” which, in essence, means that the more we're exposed to something, the more we tend to like it.

174
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:56,520
This happens with people we know, the advertisements we see and the songs we listen to.

175
00:22:56,520 --> 00:23:03,700
When you’re in your early teens, you probably spend a fair amount of time listening to music or watching music videos.

176
00:23:03,700 --> 00:23:08,200
Your favorite songs and artists become familiar, comforting parts of your routine.

177
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:18,040
For many people over 30, job and family obligations increase, so there’s less time to spend discovering new music.

178
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:25,920
Instead, many will simply listen to old, familiar favorites from that period of their lives when they had more free time.

179
00:23:25,920 --> 00:23:31,280
Of course those teen years weren’t necessarily carefree.

180
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:37,680
They’re famously confusing, which is why so many TV shows and movies revolve around high school turmoil.

181
00:23:37,680 --> 00:23:46,600
Psychology research has shown that the emotions that we experience as teens seem more intense than those that come later.

182
00:23:46,600 --> 00:23:54,020
And we also know that intense emotions are associated with stronger memories and preferences.

183
00:23:54,020 --> 00:24:01,420
Both of these might explain why the songs we listen to during this period become so memorable and beloved.

184
00:24:01,420 --> 00:24:05,860
So there’s nothing wrong with your parents because they don’t like your music.

185
00:24:05,860 --> 00:24:18,360
Rather it’s all part of the natural order of things.

186
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:36,880
Q22. What does the speaker mainly discites in this talk?

187
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:57,220
Q23. What have studies found about most people by the time they turn 33?

188
00:24:57,220 --> 00:25:17,380
Q24. What do we learn from one of the most researched laws of social psychology?

189
00:25:17,380 --> 00:25:40,507
Q25. What might explain the fact that songs people listen to in their teen years are memorable and beloved?
